s TD equity \u2014 LV surrender 0.44 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone LV throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Tre Tucker</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to KC \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (23 carries, 100 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though KC's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>12 targets and 103 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 69 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>LV \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LV are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (46% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 LV 